      "text": "To find the largest prime divisor of \\(11! + 12!\\), we start by expressing the given expression in a more manageable form. Recall that \\(12! = 12 \\times 11!\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[\n11! + 12! = 11! + 12 \\times 11! = 11!(1 + 12) = 11! \\times 13\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the prime divisors of \\(11! \\times 13\\). The prime divisors of \\(11!\\) are the primes less than or equal to 11, which are 2, 3, 5, 7, and 11. The prime divisor of \\(13\\) is 13. Therefore, the prime divisors of \\(11! \\times 13\\) are 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, and 13.\n\nAmong these, the largest prime divisor is 13. Thus, the largest prime divisor of \\(11! + 12!\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]",
